A convenient, oven-free variation of scalloped potatoes prepared entirely in one skillet on the stovetop. Thinly sliced potatoes cook tender in simmering cream and chicken broth before being capped with melted cheddar.

*Adapted from [Rachel Cooks](https://www.rachelcooks.com/stovetop-scalloped-potatoes/#wprm-recipe-container-35071).*

## Ingredients

- 1 tablespoon unsalted butter
- 2 pounds potatoes, thinly sliced (about ⅛-inch slices)
- ½ medium onion, thinly sliced into half-moons
- ¾ cup reduced-sodium chicken broth (or vegetable broth)
- ½ cup heavy cream
- ½ teaspoon kosher salt
- ¼ teaspoon coarse ground black pepper
- Pinch freshly grated nutmeg (or ground nutmeg)
- 1 ¼ cups shredded sharp cheddar cheese
- Fresh chives, chopped for garnish (optional)

## Instructions

1. **Sweat the Potatoes and Onions:** Melt the butter in a wide lidded skillet over medium heat. Add the sliced potato rounds and half-moon onions, tossing gently for 5 minutes so the vegetables soften slightly without taking on heavy browning.
2. **Whisk the Braising Liquid:** In a measuring cup or small bowl, whisk together the chicken broth, heavy cream, salt, coarse black pepper, and nutmeg until blended.
3. **Cover and Simmer:** Pour the cream mixture over the vegetables in the pan. Increase heat to medium-high to establish a boil, then drop heat down to low, cover with a tight-fitting lid, and cook for 13 to 15 minutes. The potatoes should pierce easily with a fork and the simmering cream will reduce into a velvety sauce.
4. **Melt the Cheddar:** Scatter the shredded cheddar evenly across the surface. Replace the lid and cook over low heat for 2 to 3 minutes until the cheese is completely melted and bubbly.
5. **Broil for Color (Optional):** If cooking in an oven-safe skillet like cast iron, place the uncovered pan beneath a preheated high broiler for 2 to 3 minutes to blister and toast the cheese crust.
6. **Rest and Serve:** Remove from the burner and allow the skillet to rest undisturbed for 5 to 10 minutes, which lets the starches settle and thickens the sauce. Scatter fresh chives over the top and serve warm.

## Notes

- **Potato Variety:** Yukon Golds or Russets work best. Yukon Golds hold their shape and have a buttery texture, while Russets break down slightly to create an even thicker sauce.
- **Mandoline Slicer:** A mandoline makes quick, even ⅛-inch slices so all potatoes cook at the same rate.
